      "hints": [
        "Set the limit of the iterative sequence to $x$, giving $x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{5 - 2x}}$.",
        "Square both sides, cross-multiply, and rearrange into standard polynomial form to show $2x^3 - 5x^2 + 4 = 0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Assume the iterative sequence converges to a limit $x$. At the limit, $x_{n+1} = x_n = x$:\n$$x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{5 - 2x}}$$",
        "Square both sides of the equation:\n$$x^2 = \\dfrac{4}{5 - 2x}$$",
        "Multiply through by the denominator $(5 - 2x)$:\n$$x^2(5 - 2x) = 4$$\n$$5x^2 - 2x^3 = 4$$",
        "Rearrange all terms to one side:\n$$2x^3 - 5x^2 + 4 = 0$$",
        "Notice that the left-hand side is exactly $f(x)$. Hence, if the sequence converges, it converges to a root of $f(x) = 0$."
      ],
      "checking": {
